<section xml:id="sec-loops-nested">
  <title>Nested Loops</title>

	<introduction>
  <p>
    A <term>nested loop</term> is a loop inside another loop.
    Nested loops appear naturally when you need to work over a grid, a table, or the entries of a matrix.
    The outer loop controls one index (like the row), and the inner loop controls another (like the column).
  </p>
	</introduction>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-nested-building-matrix">
    <title>Building a Matrix Entry-by-Entry</title>

    <p>
      The following example builds a multiplication table matrix, where <code>M(i,j) = i*j</code>.
      Preallocate first, then fill entries.
    </p>

    <program language="matlab">
			m = 5;
			n = 6;

			M = zeros(m,n);

			for i = 1:m
					for j = 1:n
							M(i,j) = i*j;
					end
			end

			M
    </program>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-nested-searching">
    <title>Searching for a Condition in a Grid</title>

    <p>
      Nested loops can also search for an entry that satisfies a condition.
      In practice, MATLAB’s vectorized tools often replace this, but writing it once helps you understand
      the underlying mechanics.
    </p>

    <program language="matlab">
A = randi(20, 4, 6);

found = false;

for i = 1:size(A,1)
    for j = 1:size(A,2)
        if A(i,j) == 17
            found = true;
            location = [i, j];
            break
        end
    end
    if found
        break
    end
end
    </program>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-nested-cost">
    <title>Efficiency and Alternatives</title>

    <p>
      Nested loops can be computationally expensive when arrays are large because the total number of iterations
      grows like (rows) times (columns). MATLAB often provides vectorized operations that are faster and shorter.
      Still, nested loops remain useful when the update rule is complicated or when you are implementing an algorithm
      step-by-step.
    </p>
  </subsection>

	<subsection xml:id="sec-nested-loops">
		<title>Nested Loops</title>

		<subtitle>Repeating a Repeat</subtitle>

		<introduction>
		<p>
			Sometimes the task you need to repeat also contains repetition. That is when you use a
			<term>nested loop</term>: a loop inside another loop. The outer loop controls the “bigger” repetition,
			and for each outer iteration, the inner loop runs through its own full set of iterations.
		</p>

		<p>
			A typical nested-loop skeleton looks like this:
		</p>

		<program language="matlab">
	for k = 1:6

			% Perform the "j" loop for each k
			for j = 1:3

					% Do something for each k and j

			end

	end
		</program>

		<p>
			This example shows nested <c>for</c>-loops, but the same idea applies to <c>while</c>-loops. You can even mix
			a <c>for</c>-loop on the outside with a <c>while</c>-loop on the inside (or vice versa) when it fits the situation.
		</p>
		</introduction>


		<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-nested-example-1">
			<title>Example 1: Predicting Printed Output</title>

			<p>
				The simplest way to understand nesting is to watch the order of execution.
				In the loop below, the inner loop runs completely for <c>k = 1</c>, then runs completely again for <c>k = 2</c>.
			</p>

			<program language="matlab">
	for k = 1:2
			for j = [2 5 1]
					fprintf('k = %g and j = %g \n', k, j)
			end
	end
			</program>

			<p>
				This prints six lines total, because there are 2 outer iterations and 3 inner iterations per outer iteration:
				<m>2 \times 3 = 6</m>.
			</p>
		</subsubsection>

		<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-nested-example-2">
			<title>Example 2: A Two-Index Computation Without Arrays</title>

			<p>
				Your original demo used nested loops to process a table of values entry-by-entry.
				Since we are postponing arrays, we will keep the same core idea—two indices, two levels of repetition—but
				use a purely scalar computation.
			</p>

			<p>
				In the code below, think of <c>r</c> and <c>c</c> as “row” and “column” counters that identify a position
				in a conceptual grid. For each pair <c>(r,c)</c>, we compute a scalar value and print it.
			</p>

			<program language="matlab">
	for r = 1:4
			for c = 1:3

					% A scalar computation that depends on both indices
					value = r^2 - 2*c + 1;

					fprintf('(r,c) = (%g,%g), value = %g \n', r, c, value)

			end
	end
			</program>

			<p>
				Even without storing results, this example demonstrates the main nested-loop habit:
				the inner loop runs through all <c>c</c>-values for a fixed <c>r</c>, then <c>r</c> advances and the process repeats.
			</p>
		</subsubsection>

		<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-nested-example-3">
			<title>Example 3: Counting How Often a Condition Happens</title>

			<p>
				Another classic nested-loop task is counting how many times something happens while scanning over
				a two-index space. When arrays are introduced, the “something” is often “this entry equals zero.”
				For now, we can practice the same skill by generating a scalar value from <c>(r,c)</c> and counting
				how often it equals zero.
			</p>

			<p>
				The function below counts the number of index pairs <c>(r,c)</c> in an <c>n_rows</c>-by-<c>n_cols</c> grid
				for which a particular scalar expression evaluates to 0.
			</p>

			<program language="matlab">
	function count = zero_count_grid(n_rows, n_cols)

			count = 0;

			for r = 1:n_rows
					for c = 1:n_cols

							% A scalar "cell value" defined by r and c
							cell_value = r - c;

							if cell_value == 0
									count = count + 1;
							end

					end
			end

	end
			</program>

			<p>
				Test it on a few sizes. For example, on a 4-by-3 grid, <c>cell_value = r - c</c> equals 0 exactly when <c>r = c</c>,
				so the count should be the number of matching pairs that fit in that rectangle.
			</p>

			<program language="matlab">
	count1 = zero_count_grid(4,3)
	count2 = zero_count_grid(10,10)
			</program>
		</subsubsection>

		<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-nested-loop-takeaways">
			<title>Takeaways</title>

			<ul marker="square">
				<li>
					A nested loop is a loop inside a loop. The inner loop completes all of its iterations for each iteration
					of the outer loop.
				</li>
				<li>
					Nested loops naturally model processes indexed by two counters (like <c>r</c> and <c>c</c>).
				</li>
				<li>
					When arrays are introduced, nested loops become a direct way to process data stored in a grid-like structure.
					For now, you can still practice the same reasoning by computing scalar values from two indices.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</subsubsection>
	</subsection>

		<reading-questions xml:id="rqs-">
			<exercise xml:id="activity-for-loop-drill-4">
				<title>Rewrite repeated <pf>fprintf</pf> code: stars</title>

				<task xml:id="activity-for-loop-drill-4a">
					<statement>
						<p>
							The code below has repeated lines:
							<program language="matlab">
								fprintf('*\n');
								fprintf('**\n');
								fprintf('***\n');
								fprintf('****\n');
							</program>
							Rewrite it as a <pf>for</pf>-loop that prints one more star on each line.
						</p>
					</statement>
					<solution component="solution">
						<program language="matlab">
							stars = '';
							for k = 1:4
								stars = [stars '*'];
								fprintf('%s\n', stars);
							end
						</program>
					</solution>
				</task>
			</exercise>
		</reading-questions>

		<reading-questions xml:id="class-activities-nested-loops">

			<introduction>
				<p>
					These activities will help you practice using nested loops to solve interesting problems.
				</p>
			</introduction>



			


		
			<exercise xml:id="activity-perfect-numbers-nested-part">
				<title>Perfect Number Detector</title>

				<task xml:id="activity-perfect-numbers-nested-part-a">
					<statement>
						<p>
							<em>Extension:</em> The first four perfect numbers are 6, 28, 496, and 8128. Write a script using nested loops to search for all perfect numbers less than 10,000.
						</p>
					</statement>
				</task>
			</exercise>

		</reading-questions>
</section>